# Meterless rules

## Vendored engines are read-only

- `engines/` holds the Meterless reference implementations and conformance suites, vendored unchanged from the upstream commit in `engines/UPSTREAM.md`. Never edit, reformat or "fix" a file there.
- Import them as TypeScript modules through tsx, for example `import { HMEM } from "../../engines/hmem/reference/src/index.ts"`.
- Extensions live in `agent/src/` and must keep the engine contract: the H-MEM facade (`agent/src/memory/hmemFacade.ts`, a subclass of `HMEM`) has to pass `engines/hmem/conformance/runner.ts` via `HMEM_IMPL=<absolute path, forward slashes>`.
- Rerun conformance after any change that touches an engine boundary and record the result in `docs/conformance.md`. `npm install` inside `engines/` rewrites lock files: restore with `git checkout -- engines`.

## H-MEM

- `new HMEM({ persistDir: "data/hmem", actor: "cw-agent", minerFn })`. `minerFn` calls Nemotron with think off and strict JSON array output; the reference's model-free path is the fallback.
- Every tool result becomes fact records with provenance `{zetaris_object, sql_sha256, endpoint, licence, retrieved_at, latency_ms, cache_state}` and `source: "zetaris:<object>"`. Tags: `dataset:<catalogue id>`, `column:<object>.<column>`, `key:<left>-><right>`, `utility:<cik10>`, `state:<XX>`, `declaration:<disasterNumber>`, `station:<id>`, `period:<CYyyyyQn>`.
- `key_verified` memories (a measured proposal with its rate, orphans and SQL sha256) and `source_access` memories (a source registered, probed, or found failing) persist across runs; a warm run skips re-measuring verified keys and counts the skipped chunks. Provenance is refreshed on reconfirm (latest retrieval wins, previous kept in a `history` list capped at 5).
- Seed at boot from the getting-started `open_data/manifest.json` (its `known_to_fail` sources become negative memories), the vendor's ISSUE notes, and this project's own pre-checks (`seeds/catalogue.json`: frames serve the latest-filed fact, `CAST(val AS DOUBLE)`, `LPAD` on cik, the Q_FLAG rule, the 9 s filestore read cost, OpenFEMA `$filter` percent-encoding, `metadata.count` is not a row count). `why(factId)` returns the ledger history for the UI.
- Dream proposals materialise only through the analyst's `approve()`; an approved invariant may only tighten a verdict (lower a confidence label, add a Not-tested item), never loosen it.

## World Model

- `new WorldModel({ storage: "local", namespace: "crosswalk", directory: "data/world", conflictPolicy: "most-recent-wins" })`.
- Entities: dataset (`catalogue:<id>`), column (`<object>.<column>`), key, utility (`sec:<cik10>`), state, declaration (`fema:<disasterNumber>`), station (`ghcn:<id>`), quarter. Relationships: `crosswalks_to` (left column to right column, with `transform`, `match_rate`, `verdict`, validFrom = the measurement time), `serves` (utility to state), `declared_in` (declaration to state), `located_in` (station to state), `registered_as` (dataset to Zetaris object).
- Provenance `at` comes from the source record (FEMA `declarationDate`, SEC `filed`, GHCN `DATE`) where one exists and from the measurement time for keys, so re-ingest is idempotent (empty diff; reference check).
- The vendored `control-plane/live.html` shows only its own seed data; never present it as showing Crosswalk data.

## Markovian

- `new Markovian({ chunkConfig: { chunkSize: 2000, maxChunks: 12, carryoverTokens: 800, overlapTokens: 0 }, historyDir: "data/markovian", generator })`.
- The orchestrator is unchanged; our generator (`agent/src/loop/generator.ts`) runs at most 2 tool calls per chunk and returns marker text: content, one `<NEEDS_TOOL tool=".." input='..'/>` per executed call, `[STATE_CHECKPOINT]` with the typed state JSON, and `[TASK_COMPLETE]` only when the checklist is satisfied.
- Checklist: sources_found, shapes_probed, keys_proposed, keys_measured, sources_registered, usl_built, dq_run, answered, not_tested_listed, note_written. The fallback step is the next open checklist item. A run that ends at max-chunks yields a withheld answer with reason "discovery incomplete".
- Planner `num_predict` 3072, think on; a reply that is empty with done_reason `length` is repaired once with think off; the memo job runs think off.
- Telemetry labels: Ollama `prompt_eval_count`/`eval_count` are "measured"; the naive full-history baseline is "estimated (chars/4)".

## Scout Intent (our partial reimplementation)

- There is no upstream reference. Our subset (`agent/src/contract/`) is labelled "partial reimplementation" everywhere it is described.
- Guard: scan every untrusted text (filer names, SIC descriptions, FEMA designated areas, catalogue titles) before any model sees it; on a hit wrap it in `<untrusted>` and raise the flag `untrusted_text`. Never drop text silently.
- Execution contract: HMAC-SHA256 over canonical JSON with `CW_CONTRACT_SECRET`, policy pack `crosswalk-scout-subset@1`, surfaces `cw-ui` and `cw-agent`, expiry 15 minutes, one-shot use. Reads are low risk; the registrar plans (`register_source` and the inherited `register_frames`, `register_concept`, `register_submission`) are medium risk; an analyst override is high risk and needs an `analyst-approval` with a reason on the `cw-ui` surface. Verified by both the TypeScript side and the Python registrar (`gateway/contracts.py`), which share the test vector in `agent/test/fixtures/contract-vector.ts` and `gateway/tests/test_contracts.py`. Describe it as an integrity check, not cryptographic-grade security.
